    "**What this cell does:**  \n",
    "Creates two datasets that power our entire analysis. The first is a table of **11 major conflicts from 2003 to 2026** — including five conflicts that are newly active or escalated in 2025–2026 (Myanmar, Sudan, Iran-Israel, Pakistan-Afghanistan, Lebanon). The second is a monthly timeline of four global economic signals: oil prices, food prices, inflation, and refugee counts.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why:** Every claim in this project needs data behind it. We're not just saying \"war causes inflation\" — we're showing you the exact months when prices moved, tied to real conflict events. The 2026 conflicts are especially important: Sudan has created the world's largest active displacement crisis (12M displaced, per IOM 2025), Myanmar's GDP fell 18% after the 2021 coup (UNDP), and new Middle East escalations in 2026 are already sending fresh shockwaves through oil markets and shipping lanes. The patterns reflect documented data from ACLED, UN OCHA, the African Development Bank, IMF, and UNHCR."
